;;; S1.21.8 D3 / M1a S1a.4.1 — a guard the lookahead cannot judge pre-fork.
;;;
;;; The companion to 13_lookahead_naf_world.ein. That file is the case the
;;; lookahead *can* decide in the world `kb` plus `h`: the guard reads the
;;; candidate's own relation as a positive premise, so re-running it seeded
;;; at `h` answers exactly. This file is the case it **cannot**.
;;;
;;; `premature-signoff`'s guard nests one absent inside another. Read from
;;; the inside out:
;;;
;;;   (absent (and (needs-review ?y) (absent (signed ?y))))
;;;       -- ?y has no unmet review need: "?y is review-clear"
;;;   (absent (and (pending ?y) <review-clear ?y>))
;;;       -- no pending item is review-clear
;;;
;;; so the rule reads "you signed something while nothing pending was ready",
;;; and it is a contradiction.
;;;
;;; A nested absent is **non-monotone**: adding a fact can make the inner
;;; absent fail, which makes the outer one *pass*, which adds a match the
;;; two cheap checks cannot see. Here `(signed B)` is exactly such a fact —
;;; it makes B review-clear, so the guard fails and the rule does not fire —
;;; yet `kb` alone has no review-clear pending item, and `(signed B)` is not
;;; a positive premise of the guard's query, so seeding it finds nothing
;;; either. Judged by those two checks the guard "passes", the simulation
;;; derives `(false)`, and a live hypothesis is killed.
;;;
;;; The lookahead therefore refuses to judge a disjunct whose guards contain
;;; a nested absent and skips it. That only ever loses a kill — the branch
;;; forks and dies normally — which is the safe direction for a filter whose
;;; contract is that it may never report a live hypothesis as dead.
;;;
;;; The depth is the point, and the file cannot be simplified to a `forall`.
;;; At **one** level of nesting the two cheap checks are already exact — a
;;; blocked binding stays blocked, because the inner query is positive and
;;; adding a fact can only give it more matches — so the skip is inert and a
;;; `forall`-shaped fixture would pass either way. It takes **two** levels
;;; for the flip to reach the outer absent.
;;;
;;; And the kill does not merely skip a branch: it is cached as
;;; `(not (signed B))` in the root, so `auto-sign`'s later derivation of
;;; `(signed B)` dies against it, every branch dies, and the verdict flips.
;;;
;;; Measured with `ein solve -e -s`, 2026-08-18:
;;;
;;;   as shipped                          Solution, ?x = B, 2 enterings
;;;   nested guard judged eagerly         Contradiction, k = 0, 0 enterings

(config :enable-pre-branch-lookahead true)

(relation pending      T)
(relation needs-review T)
(relation waived       T)
(relation signed       T)

(rule auto-sign ()
  :match  (and (pending ?y) (waived ?y))
  :assert (signed ?y)
  :why    "{?y} is waived, so it is signed"
  :priority 100)

(rule premature-signoff ()
  :match  (and (signed ?x)
               (absent (and (pending ?y)
                            (absent (and (needs-review ?y)
                                         (absent (signed ?y)))))))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "signed {?x} while nothing pending was review-clear"
  :priority 200)

(pending      B :source "(1)")
(needs-review B :source "(2)")

(query
  :goal (signed ?x))
